  {
   "h": "Carthage after Zama",
   "body": "<p>After Scipio defeated Hannibal at Zama in 202 BC, Carthage accepted peace in 201 on hard terms. It gave up Spain and its other overseas possessions, surrendered all but ten of its warships and its war elephants, paid an indemnity of 10,000 talents spread over fifty years, and promised not to make war without Rome's permission.</p><p>Hannibal stayed in public life. In 196 BC he was elected one of the two suffetes, the annual chief magistrates of Carthage, and used the office to attack the corruption of the ruling families and to put the city's finances in order. His success made him powerful enemies at home, and they were quick to tell Rome that he was plotting a new war.</p>"
